============================================================\
THE MENTAL MAP \'97 WHY LAYERS EXIST\
[DURING VIDEO]\
============================================================\
\
Networks group different jobs into [  layer                  ] based on what each\
one is responsible for.\
\
Each layer uses the services of the layer [          before or below it         ] it,\
and provides services to the layer [         after           ] it.\
\
The full stack of protocols working together is called the\
[      network              ] stack.\
\
This matters in DevOps because every tool you use \'97 Docker, Nginx,\
AWS security groups, load balancers \'97 operates at a specific layer.\
Knowing the layer tells you what the tool can see and what it can't.\
\
------------------------------------------------------------\
THE 5 LAYERS \'97 FILL IN AS JEREMY INTRODUCES THEM\
[DURING VIDEO]\
------------------------------------------------------------\
\
Layer 5 \'97 [        Application            ] Layer\
  Job: app-to-app communication, formatting and interpreting data\
  Examples: HTTP, HTTPS, SSH, DNS, FTP\
  DevOps tools at this layer: web servers, APIs, browsers\
\
Layer 4 \'97 [           Transport         ] Layer\
  Job: process-to-process delivery using [         Port           ] numbers\
  Examples: TCP, UDP\
  DevOps tools at this layer: load balancers, firewalls filtering by port\
\
Layer 3 \'97 [         Internet            ] Layer  (also called: Network Layer)\
  Job: host-to-host delivery using [         IP           ] addresses\
  Examples: IP (v4 and v6), ICMP\
  DevOps tools at this layer: routers, route tables, VPCs, subnetting\
\
Layer 2 \'97 [         Local Network            ] Layer  (also called: Data Link Layer)\
  Job: hop-to-hop delivery on a local network using [          MAC        ] addresses\
  Examples: Ethernet, Wi-Fi\
  DevOps tools at this layer: switches, VLANs, ARP\
\
Layer 1 \'97 [        Physical            ] Layer\
  Job: sending bits as electrical, optical, or radio signals over the physical medium\
  Examples: copper cable, fiber, Wi-Fi radio\
  DevOps relevance: AWS handles this for you in cloud. Understand it exists.\
\
------------------------------------------------------------\
NOTE ON LAYER NUMBERING\
------------------------------------------------------------\
\
AWS docs, certifications, and most tools use OSI layer numbers, not TCP/IP.\
The TCP/IP Application layer = OSI Layer [   7     ].\
When AWS says "Layer 7 load balancer" \'97 that means [         Application           ] layer.\
When AWS says "Layer 4 load balancer" \'97 that means [          transport          ] layer.\
\
============================================================\
IP ADDRESSES vs MAC ADDRESSES vs PORT NUMBERS\
[DURING VIDEO]\
============================================================\
\
These three address types live at three different layers.\
Confusing them is one of the most common junior engineer mistakes.\
\
IP Address   \uc0\u8594  Layer [    3    ] \u8594  identifies a [    host/interface                ] on the network\
              scope: [       Global             ] (works across networks)\
\
MAC Address  \uc0\u8594  Layer [    2    ] \u8594  identifies an [         NIC           ] on a local network\
              scope: [         Local/Physical           ] (only works on the local network segment)\
\
Port Number  \uc0\u8594  Layer [    4    ] \u8594  identifies a [          process/service          ] running on a host\
              examples: port [   80     ] = HTTP, port [    443    ] = HTTPS, port [    22    ] = SSH\
\
\
